lysis is sufficient for TICA / dihedral computation of tetrapeptides (small systems); but trajectory parsing tools need to be compiled / wheels provided; no CUDA inference for generative models; the large core count helps for per-system parallel bootstrapping.\n\nPart 2: Conceptual design.\n\n2.1 Collective variables:\n- Backbone dihedrals: per residue phi (C'-CA-C-N... standard definitions), psi, omega (restricted to trans except Pro cis); use MDTraj/MDAnalysis atom naming conventions (phi: C(i-1)-N(i)-CA(i)-CA(i-1)... the standard is: phi = C(i-1), N(i), CA(i), CA(i)... Actually phi: atoms C(i-1)-N(i)-CA(i)-C(i); psi: N(i)-CA(i)-C(i)-N(i+1)). For capped tetrapeptides: 3 phi/psi pairs (residues 2-4 for phi, 1-3 for psi); report per-residue.\n- Side-chain chi angles: chi1..chin per residue by IUPAC convention; exclude Gly; Pro has chi1-chi2 restricted in ring — handle specially (report but flag ring constraint, range approximately ±30°); include residues with rotatable bonds; also include a \"no rotatable side chain\" class (Gly, and possibly Ala as pseudo-rotation of methyl? treat Ala chi3 as a 3-fold symmetric methyl rotor, or exclude). Handle both rotatable and non-rotatable side-chain classes as requested.\n- Periodicity: all dihedrals are on S^1; use wrapped differences (map to [-π, π)); circular statistics: circular mean/variance, circular standard deviation, circular KL via von Mises mixture or 2D torus KDE; never take naive differences across the wrap.\n- TICA: input features = time-lagged (concatenated t and t+τ? no —) TICA input: feature vector over time = all backbone + side-chain dihedrals as sin/cos pairs (or cos/sin transformed), possibly pairwise distances; lag time τ selected by implied timescale plateau check on the longer side of the pair (τ sweep 1–100 frames); fit the TICA model on ground truth only (its means, covariances C(0), C(τ), eigenvectors), then project both the ground truth and sampled trajectories onto the same projection. Number of components k selected by cumulative timescale/variance criterion (e.g., all components with implied timescale > 10× lag time, cap k at 10). Report eigenvalues and variance fractions.\n- Sampling trajectory frames: for generative samples that may be unsorted / independent samples rather than Markov chains: treat as equilibrium ensemble; TICA is only meaningful for MD series; for unsorted sample collections, use \"kinetic map\"? More simply: if samples are independent configurations, compare static distributions in GT-fit TICA space (linear projection is still a well-defined feature map; interpret it as a dimension reduction, not a kinetic decomposition). Also, for samples without kinetic ordering, the projection becomes a fixed nonlinear-free feature map — acceptable; make this explicit.\n- Alignment: superpose on reference (backbone alignment) to remove rotation/translation — MDTraj superpose to the same reference frame for both sets; needed if sampled coordinates are not pre-aligned.\n\n2.2 Similarity metrics:\n- Univariate circular: circular Earth Mover / Wasserstein distance on the circle (W1 on S^1), Mardia–Jona-Lasinin test statistic for two-sample circular data /Watson's U², KS for circular data; plus KL divergence of von Mises mixture fits.\n- Kernel-based (required): Maximum Mean Discrepancy (MMD) with a periodic kernel (e.g., wrapped-Gaussian / von Mises product kernel on dihedrals, and a Gaussian kernel in TICA space); report MMD² with a permutation test (n_perm=10000, exchangeable at what unit?). Also KDE-based JS divergence using circular KDE (KULPY, circular kernel density estimator) on each dihedral with bandwidth by circular plug-in rule / cross-validation.\n- Discretized: 2D Ramachandran histograms per residue (e.g., 24×24 / 15° bins), JS divergence and chi-square on counts; a chi1 rotamer occupancy table per residue and its total-variation distance; discretization of the top TICA components (or joint kNN density ratio).\n- Joint: MMD over concatenated