ic, order permutation changes nothing (distribution is invariant); so the weak negative being on the floor is the expected behavior for a static metric, not a failure. Hmm, but the researcher's instruction is: \"prescribe the expected positions of both; if the weak negative control cannot be distinguished from the GT-vs-GT floor, the conclusion must explicitly state that the primary endpoint lacks discriminative power on this data, rather than directly declaring a pass\".\n\nWait — the intended logic seems to be: if d(GT_weakneg) ≈ floor, then the primary endpoint cannot distinguish a distributionally different but frame-reordered set... no, that's not right. Let me think about the researcher's intent: they define the weak negative as a set that should be flagged as \"not similar\" in terms of kinetics but is identical statically. For a static-distribution endpoint, being indistinguishable from the floor is correct behavior, not a lack of discriminative power. But the researcher explicitly asks for the rule: if weak negative ≈ floor → declare the primary endpoint to lack discriminative power on this data. Hmm, this contradicts theory... unless the weak negative is understood to have some effect from autocorrelation? Actually, if there is effective sample size / block bootstrap, permuting frame order makes frames independent — the distribution is the same. Metric ≈ 0.\n\nHowever, my role is: the researcher prescribes the protocol; I can faithfully record it while noting theoretical expectations. Actually, let me re-read: \"used to verify the situation where 'if similar, it indicates the static-distribution comparison lacks discriminative power'\" — hmm, perhaps the meaning is: the weak negative tests whether the metric is able to detect temporal structure; if the metric says the weak negative is \"similar\" (as expected for a static metric), then it demonstrates that the static-distribution comparison has no kinetic discriminative power — consistent with the scope limitation in section I (no claims about kinetics). Therefore expected position: weak negative ≈ floor for static metrics (this is by design, demonstrating scope limitation; for TICA/kinetic quantities, permutation breaks timescales, so kinetic diagnostics show differences).\n\nBut then \"if the weak negative control cannot be distinguished from the GT-vs-GT floor, the conclusion must explicitly state that the primary endpoint lacks discriminative power on this data\" — this states that if weak negative cannot be distinguished from the floor → must declare lack of discriminative power. Hmm, that would always be true for a static metric... Perhaps the intent is: distinguish the weak negative from the sampled set? Or does the weak negative mean permuting/resampling in a way that also slightly perturbs things? Or does \"weak negative\" mean permuting frame order, and the metric is computed after block segmentation?\n\nTo resolve: implement as follows: expected position — weak negative should be on the floor for static metrics (statically identical by construction; if the metric is computed with block-based bootstrap, order permutation only changes CI width). The \"declare lack of discriminative power\" rule applies as follows: if d(weak neg) is not distinguishable from d(GT, sampled) — no...\n\nAnother faithful reading: the rule is a guard against a degenerate endpoint. Suppose the primary endpoint is so insensitive (e.g., extremely sparse data where JS≈0 for any pair) that everything lands on the floor. Diagnostic: check whether the strong negative separates from the floor; if the strong negative also lands on the floor → endpoint has no discriminative power. And the weak negative's role: verify that the metric responds to kinetics... hmm.\n\nLet me do this. Expected positions (pre-registered):\n- Weak negative (order-permuted GT): for purely static endpoints, expected position = floor (static distribution unchanged; its role is to confirm the endpoint is static only and not contaminated by kinetics; deviation above the floor indicates the endpoint is confounded with temporal ordering — a flag).\n- TICA-based quantities: permutation destroys time correlation → expected position of kinetic diagnostics (implied timescales) is clearly above the floor; distribution over GT-fitted ICs remains on the floor.\n- Strong negative (uniform-random dihedrals; heavily-perturbed conformers): expected position is close to the maximum of the metric range, well above both floor and sampled set.\n\nAnd include the researcher's guard verbatim as a decision rule: if d(GT, weak-neg) is statistically indistinguishable from d(GT, GT-floor) and simultaneously the metric range check shows that d(weak-neg) also cannot be separated from.... Honestly, the cleanest thing is: include the researcher's rule as stated: \"if weak negative cannot be distinguished from GT-vs-GT floor, conclusion must explicitly declare: primary endpoint is insensitive to ordering/temporal-structure differences on this data (static-distribution comparison has no kinetics discriminative power) — therefore one must not extrapolate from 'sampled is similar to GT' to dynamic equivalence; if simultaneously strong negative also cannot be separated from floor, determine that the primary endpoint has no discriminative power on this data, declare all decisions for that CV invalid, rather than declaring a pass.\"\n\nThis is a coherent implementation: weak negative cannot be separated from floor = expected for a static endpoint, documented as \"no kinetic discriminative power\" (scope limitation); strong negative cannot be separated from floor = endpoint degenerate → declare invalid. This respects the intent of the researcher's sentence (must not declare a pass) while being theoretically sound. Write carefully to include the intent the researcher stated.\n\nActually, the researcher wrote \"if the weak negative control cannot be distinguished from the GT-vs-GT floor, the conclusion must explicitly state that the primary endpoint lacks discriminative power on this data, rather than directly declaring a pass\". Implement as pre-registered reporting obligation: whenever weak negative ≈ floor, include a scope statement that the static endpoint cannot discriminate temporal-structure differences (lacks discriminative power for kinetics). And add: strong negative ≈ floor → endpoint degenerates, decision invalid. That's the honest way.\n\nV. Uncertainty — Retained items.\n\nVI. Failure/confound contingency — Existing + inconsistent directionality across systems: per-system table + system-level bootstrap pooled interval; summary mean only is not allowed; additionally report fraction of systems passing and the worst system, sign test / binomial CI against majority direction.\n\nVII. Feasibility and fallback path — CPU/RAM sufficient, no GPU, negligible cost per system; no numpy/scipy/MDTraj + no network → offline wheels needed; fallback using pure standard library (math, random, statistics, struct for XTC? Actually reading XTC/DCD in pure Python is difficult — note: fallback requires at least providing plain-text feature inputs (e.g., precomputed dihedral angle CSV) or pure-Python DCD/XTC readers (DCD is simple Fortran binary, feasible with struct; XTC needs 3fcomp compression — very difficult in pure Python → request either uncompressed DCD/TRR, or pre-extracted dihedral angles/TICA-external features). This is a good concrete feasibility note. Fallback implements circular KDE, histograms, JS, bootstrap resampling; abandons MMD permutation test or reduces to e.g. 999 permutations with a power-loss note; Watson U² implementable with standard library.\nAnd: no empirical conclusions until data + libraries are provided.\n\nVIII. Checklist of items awaiting confirmation — Which two substantively change the protocol (time ordering; independent GT replicates), the rest affect only parameter values — retain.\n\nAlso include \"decision proposition\" in the document, e.g.:\nProposition H_sim(M, s): for system s and endpoint metric d: CI_boot(d(GT, sampled)) overlaps with floor band [q_lo, q_hi] and is below strong negative − margin... Formalized:\n- Pass(s): P(d_s ≤ d_floor_max?) ... use: [d_s]_0.95upper ≤ U_floor (upper bound of floor band) AND [d_s]_0.05lower ≥?? No: lower bound of strong negative minus margin. Define floor band F = 95% interval of split-half distances; strong negative band N = 95% interval. Pass if the upper CI of d_s is below N_lo AND falls within/overlaps F (d_s CI overlaps F). Two-sided: if CI of d_s overlaps F, judged as \"similar\" (and CI is below N_lo for sanity). Also compute S score.\n- Overall answer to research question: per-system pass table + primary endpoint aggregation; answer = fraction of systems passing + pooled S with CI; no global single bit required, but report a primary judgment following pre-registered aggregation: similar on primary endpoint only if ≥2/3? No, that would be fabricating a threshold... Can specify: report per system; the aggregated claim follows the pre-registered rule of \"per-system table + system-level bootstrap mean S interval\", and the textual conclusion describes which residue classes pass. The researcher forbade mean-only reporting; that's fine.\n\nPreprocessing numerical thresholds (researcher requests concrete numbers):\n- Superposition: reference = GT backbone (N, CA, C, O + CB) mean structure after initial alignment of GT first frame; align all frames of GT and the sampled set to the same reference; Kabsch on backbone heavy atoms, mass-weighted.\n- Failure criteria: Rg deviation >15% from GT mean, or post-alignment RMSD > 4 Å (backbone heavy atoms) → mark frame alignment anomaly; fraction of anomalous frames in a trajectory >5% → system-level flag, manual inspection, exclude the system from the primary endpoint, analyze under failure contingency.\n- Geometry checks (frame-level exclusion, pre-dihedral): bond lengths for covalently bonded pairs within 0.8–1.3× template (from PDB topology); H-bonds? Keep it: bonded pairs from residue templates; nonbonded heavy-atom minimum interatomic distance ≥ 0.7 Å (hard clash) — more standard: <0.6 × sum of vdW radii and <1.0 Å? Rule to use: heavy-atom pair distance <1.0 Å → clash; planarity: absolute value of improper dihedral for sp2 peptide bond N > 30° → peptide chirality / planarity violation; CA chirality (improper sign) inverted → exclude; missing atom → fail frame. Frame exclusion; report exclusion rate per system; exclusion >10% → report valid-frame distribution comparison as primary + flag for selection bias (because exclusion is conditioned on structure → bias risk: report metric on all frames including invalid ones with dihedrals as available vs valid only, as sensitivity analysis).\n\nAlso TICA input: GT-fit, projection, sign convention, lag-time selection procedure — retained from plan.\n\nMetric table: rows = metric, columns: applicable target, single-parameter setting for primary endpoint, sensitivity grid, breakdown boundaries / caveats, equal-sample rule.\n\nSensitivity grid table: bin width {7.5, 10, 15, 20}°; KDE bandwidth {0.5, 1, 2}× rule; MMD γ {0.5, 1, 2}× median heuristic; Sinkhorn ε {0.01, 0.05, 0.1} (normalized sin/cos space); TICA τ {1, 2, 5, 10, 20, 50, 100} frames with plateau rule; k ≤10 with ITS>10τ; block length {5, 10, 20}× AC time.\n\nPrimary endpoint: 15° bin Ramachandran JS (per residue, averaged over systems), single setting, fixed.\n\nBreakdown boundaries per metric:\n- 2D 24×24 JS: biased upward under sparse occupancy; require effective count per set ≥ 500, otherwise coarse bins; report Miller–Madow-corrected JS or report raw + bias estimate; sensitive to zero-count handling (ε smoothing 0.5 pseudocount fixed).\n- Circular KDE + JS: bandwidth bias with small n (over-smoothing), multimodal von Mises mixture degeneracy; fails when distribution is concentrated near ±π? (handled by circular kernel).\n- MMD permutation test: power drops sharply under unequal n